        <text x="670" y="140" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">New Caledonia</text>
        
        <circle cx="720" cy="95" r="14" fill="#ec4899" />
        <text x="720" y="100" fill="#fff" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="bold">+12</text>
        <text x="720" y="140" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Wallis/Futuna</text>
        
        <!-- Summary Box -->
        <rect x="40" y="190" width="720" height="90" fill="var(--panel)" stroke="var(--line, #444)" rx="6" />
        <text x="60" y="215" fill="currentColor" font-size="13" font-weight="bold">Key Highlights:</text>
        <text x="60" y="240" fill="currentColor" font-size="12">• Metropolitan France operates on CET (UTC+1) / CEST (UTC+2)</text>
        <text x="60" y="260" fill="currentColor" font-size="12">• 12 total civil time zones make France the most time-zone diverse country in the world</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 1 -->
  <section id="overview-france-metropolitan" class="content-section">
    <h2>1. Overview of Metropolitan France Time Zones</h2>
    <p>
      Metropolitan France—comprising mainland France, Corsica, and small coastal islands—operates under Central European Time (CET, UTC+1) during winter and Central European Summer Time (CEST, UTC+2) during summer.
    </p>
    <p>
      For exact conversions between Paris and North American time zones, consult tools such as <a href="/cet-to-est/">CET to EST</a> and <a href="/cest-to-est/">CEST to EST</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

