  <!-- SECTION 3 -->
  <section id="history-paris-meridian" class="content-section">
    <h2>3. History of Time in France &amp; The Paris Meridian</h2>
    <p>
      Before the international adoption of Greenwich as the Prime Meridian in 1884, France used the <strong>Paris Meridian</strong> (passing through the Paris Observatory at 2°20' E) as its baseline for solar time.
    </p>
    <p>
      In 1891, France adopted Paris Mean Time as its national legal time. In 1911, France aligned its clocks with Greenwich Mean Time (UTC+0). However, during World War II in 1940, German forces shifted Metropolitan France to German Time (UTC+1). Following liberation, France opted to retain UTC+1 to keep its commercial schedule synchronized with <a href="/germany-time-zone/">Germany</a> and Central European neighbors.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4 -->
  <section id="daylight-saving-france" class="content-section">
    <h2>4. Daylight Saving Time (CEST) Rules</h2>
    <p>
      Metropolitan France re-introduced Daylight Saving Time in 1976 following the 1973 oil crisis to reduce electricity consumption for evening lighting:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Spring Shift:</strong> Last Sunday of March (clocks advance 1 hour at 02:00 CET to 03:00 CEST).</li>
      <li><strong>Autumn Shift:</strong> Last Sunday of October (clocks fall back 1 hour at 03:00 CEST to 02:00 CET).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <section id="overseas-departments-details" class="content-section">
    <h2>5. Overseas Departments &amp; Territories Time Guide</h2>
    <p>
      The French Overseas Departments (DROM-COM) maintain strict year-round time offsets to avoid local agricultural and oceanic disruption. For instance, French Guiana remains fixed at UTC-3, while Guadeloupe and Martinique maintain UTC-4 year-round.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <section id="france-vs-world-table" class="content-section">
    <h2>6. France vs World Time Zones Comparison Table</h2>
    <p>
      Compare Paris (Metropolitan France) time with key international cities:
    </p>
    <table class="data-table" style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="background: var(--panel); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);">
          <th style="padding: 10px; text-align: left;">City</th>
          <th style="padding: 10px; text-align: left;">Paris Winter (CET UTC+1)</th>
